---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifrssustainability/ifrss2.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Physical vs Transition Climate Risk" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Governance and Strategy Disclosures" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Climate Resilience — Scenario Analysis" },
        { id: "s6", label: "GHG Emissions — Scope 1, 2, and 3" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Industry-based Metrics" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Cross-Industry Metrics & Targets" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRS S2 vs TCFD and Other Frameworks" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why climate risk warranted the FIRST topic-specific standard</div>
        <div class="principle">Climate change was identified by the ISSB as the MOST developed, urgent, and broadly consistent sustainability topic across jurisdictions — building directly on the pre-existing, widely adopted TCFD recommendations. IFRS S2 applies IFRS S1's general framework SPECIFICALLY to climate-related risks and opportunities, providing detailed, industry-tailored metrics that make the Framework's relevance objective CONCRETE for this particular, financially significant risk category.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IFRS S2 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Relevance</strong> — climate risk can materially affect asset values, cost structures, and access to capital over multiple time horizons, directly relevant to investor decision-making</span>
          <span><strong>Comparability</strong> — MANDATORY GHG emissions disclosure using the internationally recognised GHG Protocol methodology enables consistent comparison ACROSS entities and industries</span>
          <span><strong>Faithful representation via scenario analysis</strong> — requiring disclosure of climate RESILIENCE under different future scenarios (rather than a single point estimate) better represents the genuine UNCERTAINTY inherent in long-term climate outcomes</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS S2 was the FIRST topic-specific ISSB standard issued (alongside IFRS S1) — reflecting the global convergence around TCFD as the most mature, broadly accepted foundation for climate disclosure standardisation.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The two fundamental risk categories</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-13-1"><span>Risk category</span><span>Nature</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Physical risk</span><span>Direct impacts from climate change itself (acute events, chronic shifts)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-13-1"><span>Transition risk</span><span>Risks arising from the SHIFT toward a lower-carbon economy (policy, technology, market changes)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">This dual classification directly mirrors the TCFD's own foundational framework, which IFRS S2 builds upon almost entirely.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRS S2 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS S2 requires an entity to disclose information about climate-related risks and opportunities that could REASONABLY BE EXPECTED to affect its cash flows, access to finance, or cost of capital over the short, medium, or long term — applied TOGETHER with IFRS S1's general requirements, using the SAME core content structure (governance, strategy, risk management, metrics and targets).</div>
        <div class="note">IFRS S2 does NOT stand alone — it is a topic-specific application of IFRS S1's overarching framework, adding climate-specific detail (scenario analysis, GHG emissions metrics, industry-based metrics) on top of the general requirements.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Physical risk</div><div class="glossary-def">Risks resulting from climate change, comprising ACUTE risks (event-driven, e.g. floods, hurricanes) and CHRONIC risks (longer-term shifts, e.g. rising sea levels, temperature changes).</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Transition risk</div><div class="glossary-def">Risks arising from efforts to transition to a lower-carbon economy, including policy, legal, technology, market, and reputational risks.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Climate resilience</div><div class="glossary-def">An entity's capacity to adjust to, or withstand, climate-related changes, developments, or uncertainties.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Physical vs Transition Climate Risk</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Two categories, each requiring distinct analysis</div>
        <div class="principle">An entity discloses BOTH physical and transition risks separately, recognising they arise from FUNDAMENTALLY different underlying drivers and require different mitigation strategies.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Distinguishing physical from transition risk for a coastal manufacturer</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A manufacturer operates a factory on a coastline vulnerable to rising sea levels (a chronic PHYSICAL risk), and also relies heavily on a carbon-intensive production process that may face increasing carbon taxes as governments tighten climate policy (a TRANSITION risk).</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The entity discloses BOTH risks SEPARATELY: the physical risk assessment addresses potential facility relocation costs or flood defence investment; the transition risk assessment addresses the potential financial impact of future carbon pricing on production costs and competitiveness — each requiring genuinely DIFFERENT strategic responses and disclosure content.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Governance and Strategy Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Applying IFRS S1's pillars specifically to climate</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Under GOVERNANCE, an entity discloses the board's oversight of climate-related risks/opportunities, and management's role in assessing/managing them. Under STRATEGY, an entity discloses the ACTUAL and POTENTIAL effects of climate-related risks/opportunities on its business model, strategy, and financial position over the short/medium/long term — including how these are factored into strategic planning and capital allocation decisions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Climate Resilience — Scenario Analysis</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely distinctive IFRS S2 requirement</div>
        <div class="principle">An entity USES CLIMATE-RELATED SCENARIO ANALYSIS to assess its climate resilience — considering AT LEAST one scenario ALIGNED with the latest international agreement on climate change (e.g. limiting global warming to 1.5°C), and (where relevant to the assessment) another scenario reflecting a MORE SEVERE climate outcome.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Applying scenario analysis</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An energy company assesses its resilience under a "1.5°C aligned" scenario (rapid transition, aggressive decarbonisation policy) versus a "higher warming" scenario (slower transition, more severe physical climate impacts).</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Under the 1.5°C scenario, the company might disclose SIGNIFICANT transition risk to its fossil fuel assets (stranded asset risk from rapid decarbonisation policy). Under the higher warming scenario, it might disclose GREATER physical risk to its infrastructure from more severe weather events — disclosing BOTH gives users a far richer picture of resilience across genuinely DIFFERENT plausible futures, rather than a single deterministic forecast.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>GHG Emissions — Scope 1, 2, and 3</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A mandatory, universally applicable metric</div>
        <div class="principle">An entity discloses its ABSOLUTE gross greenhouse gas emissions, measured in accordance with the GHG PROTOCOL methodology, separately for:</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-06-2"><span>Scope</span><span>Definition</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-06-2"><span>Scope 1</span><span>DIRECT emissions from sources the entity owns/controls</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-06-2"><span>Scope 2</span><span>INDIRECT emissions from purchased electricity, steam, heating, or cooling</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-06-2"><span>Scope 3</span><span>ALL OTHER indirect emissions occurring in the entity's VALUE CHAIN (upstream and downstream)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Categorising a manufacturer's emissions</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A manufacturer directly burns natural gas in its own factory boilers (500 tonnes CO2e), purchases grid electricity generating 300 tonnes CO2e at the power station, and its suppliers' production of raw materials generates an estimated 2,000 tonnes CO2e, while customer use of its products generates an estimated 4,500 tonnes CO2e.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Scope 1</strong> = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">500 tonnes CO2e</strong> (direct combustion, owned boilers)<br>
            <strong>Scope 2</strong> = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">300 tonnes CO2e</strong> (purchased electricity)<br>
            <strong>Scope 3</strong> = 2,000 + 4,500 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">6,500 tonnes CO2e</strong> (upstream supplier emissions + downstream customer use emissions)
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">Scope 3 emissions are typically the LARGEST category for most entities, yet also the MOST difficult to measure reliably, since they depend on data from suppliers and customers OUTSIDE the entity's direct control — a genuinely significant estimation challenge IFRS S2 explicitly acknowledges with transition relief provisions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Industry-based Metrics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">SASB-derived, industry-specific disclosure requirements</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">IFRS S2 incorporates INDUSTRY-BASED metric requirements derived substantially from the SASB Standards — recognising that climate risk manifests very DIFFERENTLY across industries (e.g. an oil and gas company's material metrics differ substantially from a software company's), and providing tailored guidance rather than a single generic checklist.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Cross-Industry Metrics & Targets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">A common baseline applicable regardless of industry</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">In addition to industry-specific metrics, IFRS S2 requires certain CROSS-INDUSTRY metrics applicable to ALL entities — including GHG emissions (Section 6), climate-related transition risks/opportunities, physical risks, capital deployment toward climate-related risks/opportunities, internal carbon pricing (if used), and remuneration linked to climate-related considerations. Entities also disclose any climate-related TARGETS set, and progress toward them.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures under IFRS S2</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Governance processes for overseeing climate-related risks/opportunities</span>
          <span>Strategy, including effects on business model, and climate resilience via scenario analysis</span>
          <span>Risk management processes for identifying/assessing/monitoring climate-related risks</span>
          <span>Scope 1, 2, and 3 GHG emissions, plus cross-industry and industry-based metrics</span>
          <span>Climate-related targets set, and progress toward achieving them</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Which physical and transition risks are material to the specific entity</span>
              <span>Selecting appropriate climate scenarios for resilience assessment</span>
              <span>Determining the boundary of the entity's value chain for Scope 3 purposes</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Scope 3 emissions, heavily reliant on third-party data and estimation methodologies</span>
              <span>Financial effects of climate risk under different scenarios</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">Scope 3 emissions measurement is widely regarded as the SINGLE most challenging aspect of IFRS S2 implementation, given the reliance on data quality and availability from entities OUTSIDE the reporting entity's direct control — IFRS S2 includes specific transition relief allowing additional time to build reliable Scope 3 measurement capability.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS S2 vs TCFD and Other Frameworks</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IFRS S2 vs TCFD and the EU's ESRS climate standard</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS S2</span><span>TCFD / EU ESRS E1</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Foundational structure</span><span>DIRECTLY built on, and consistent with, TCFD's four pillars</span><span>TCFD itself; ESRS E1 shares many similar concepts but with EU-specific additions</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Materiality basis</span><span>SINGLE (financial/enterprise value) materiality</span><span>ESRS applies DOUBLE materiality — also requiring disclosure of the entity's climate IMPACT, not just risk exposure</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>GHG emissions scope</span><span>Scope 1, 2, and 3 mandatory (with Scope 3 transition relief)</span><span>Similarly comprehensive under ESRS, though with somewhat different specific methodological requirements</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS S2 is widely regarded as ESSENTIALLY codifying and formalising TCFD into a mandatory IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standard — many jurisdictions ADOPTING IFRS S2 explicitly reference it as their formal replacement for voluntary TCFD reporting.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRS S2 disclosure lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Disclosure item</span><span>Location</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Governance, strategy, risk management</span><span><span class="badge bp">Sustainability Report</span></span><span>Part of general purpose financial reporting</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Scenario analysis and climate resilience assessment</span><span><span class="badge bp">Sustainability Report</span></span><span>At least one 1.5°C-aligned scenario required</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Scope 1, 2, 3 GHG emissions</span><span><span class="badge bp">Sustainability Report</span></span><span>GHG Protocol methodology; Scope 3 subject to transition relief</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Industry-based and cross-industry metrics</span><span><span class="badge bp">Sustainability Report</span></span><span>SASB-derived, tailored by industry</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Financial effects reflected in recognised amounts (where material)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Via ordinary IFRS Accounting Standards (e.g. IAS 36, IAS 37)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Like IFRS S1, IFRS S2 is a DISCLOSURE standard — its metrics and narrative sit within the sustainability-related financial disclosures, explicitly connected to (but distinct from) the financial statements themselves.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS S1</span><span>Supplies the general framework (materiality, four pillars, connected information) that IFRS S2 applies specifically to climate</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 36</span><span>Climate transition risk may affect impairment testing assumptions for long-lived, carbon-intensive assets</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 37</span><span>Climate-related legal/regulatory risks may give rise to provisions or contingent liabilities</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 16 / IAS 38</span><span>Useful life estimates for assets may need reassessment in light of climate transition plans</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">GHG Protocol</div><div class="glossary-def">The internationally recognised methodology for measuring and reporting greenhouse gas emissions, mandated by IFRS S2 for Scope 1/2/3 disclosures.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Internal carbon price</div><div class="glossary-def">A price an entity applies internally to its own greenhouse gas emissions, used in investment decision-making — disclosed as a cross-industry metric if used.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "GHG Protocol", definition: "The internationally recognised methodology for measuring and reporting greenhouse gas emissions, mandated by IFRS S2 for Scope 1/2/3 disclosures." },
      { term: "Internal carbon price", definition: "A price an entity applies internally to its own greenhouse gas emissions, used in investment decision-making." },
      { term: "Physical risk", definition: "Risks resulting from climate change, comprising acute and chronic risks." },
      { term: "Transition risk", definition: "Risks arising from efforts to transition to a lower-carbon economy." },
      { term: "Climate resilience", definition: "An entity's capacity to adjust to, or withstand, climate-related changes, developments, or uncertainties." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRSS2} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>



